## Every Command Explained

### Phase 1: Checking Current State

#### `git status`
```bash
git status
```
**What it does:** Shows the current state of your working directory — which files are modified, staged, or untracked.

**Concept:** Git tracks files in 3 areas:
```
Working Directory  →  Staging Area  →  Repository
 (your edits)        (git add)        (git commit)
```
`git status` tells you where each file currently sits.

**Our output showed:**
- `M backend/src/config/cloudinary.ts` → **M**odified but not staged
- `?? backend/src/config/Gemini.ts` → **??** = Untracked (new file Git doesn't know about)

---

#### `git remote -v`
```bash
git remote -v
```
**What it does:** Shows which remote servers your repo is connected to.

**Concept:** A "remote" is a copy of your repo on a server (GitHub). `-v` means "verbose" — shows both fetch and push URLs.

**Our output:** `origin https://github.com/Abhinav27112005/Hackathon.git`
- `origin` = default name for your GitHub remote

---

#### `git branch`
```bash
git branch      # local branches only
git branch -a   # all branches (local + remote)
```
**What it does:** Lists all branches. The `*` marks which branch you're currently on.

**Concept:** Branches are like parallel timelines. Each branch can have different code without affecting others.

---

#### `git status --porcelain` / `git status --short`
```bash
git status --short
```
**What it does:** Compact version of `git status`. Shows file status in 2-character codes:
- `M ` = modified and staged
- ` M` = modified but NOT staged
- `??` = untracked (new file)
- `A ` = newly added to staging
- `D ` = deleted

---

### Phase 2: Saving Your Work Temporarily

#### `git stash`
```bash
git stash
```
**What it does:** Temporarily saves all your uncommitted changes and gives you a clean working directory.

**Concept:** Think of it as putting your changes in a "pocket" so you can switch branches safely. Your changes aren't lost — they're saved in a stash stack.

**Why we used it:** We needed to switch to `main` branch to create a new branch, but you had uncommitted changes. Git won't let you switch branches with uncommitted changes that would conflict.

```
Before stash:  Working Directory has changes
After stash:   Working Directory is clean (changes saved in stash)
```

---

### Phase 3: Creating the New Branch

#### `git checkout main`
```bash
git checkout main
```
**What it does:** Switches to the `main` branch. All files in your editor change to match `main`'s code.

**Concept:** `checkout` means "switch to this branch and update my files to match."

> [!NOTE]
> We initially tried branching from `main`, but this caused conflicts because your scheme code depends on files that only exist in `feature/voice-profile-ai`. So we switched strategy.

---

#### `git checkout -b feature/scheme-pdf-rag`
```bash
git checkout -b feature/scheme-pdf-rag
```
**What it does:** Creates a **new** branch AND switches to it in one command.

**It's a shortcut for:**
```bash
git branch feature/scheme-pdf-rag    # create branch
git checkout feature/scheme-pdf-rag  # switch to it
```

**Concept:** The `-b` flag means "create new **b**ranch." The new branch starts as a copy of whatever branch you were on.

---

### Phase 4: Handling the Conflict

#### What went wrong (and how we fixed it)

```bash
# First attempt: created branch from main
git checkout main
git checkout -b feature/scheme-pdf-rag
git stash pop  # ❌ CONFLICT!
```

**Why it conflicted:** Your stashed changes modified files like `cloudinary.ts` and [schemeController.ts](file:///w:/NPTEL%20INTERNSHIP/Hackathon/NitiSetu/backend/src/controllers/schemeController.ts) that don't exist on `main`. When Git tried to apply your changes, it couldn't find the files to modify.

**The fix:**
```bash
# Reset the conflicted state
git reset HEAD                           # unstage everything
git checkout -- .                        # discard working directory changes
git clean -fd                            # remove untracked files

# Delete the bad branch
git checkout feature/voice-profile-ai    # go back to original branch
git branch -D feature/scheme-pdf-rag     # delete the branch

# Create branch from the RIGHT place
git stash pop                            # restore our changes
git checkout -b feature/scheme-pdf-rag   # create branch from HERE
```

#### Commands used during cleanup:

| Command | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| `git reset HEAD` | Unstages all files (moves them from staging back to working directory) |
| `git checkout -- .` | Discards all modifications in working directory (restores to last commit) |
| `git clean -fd` | Deletes untracked files. `-f` = force, `-d` = include directories |
| `git branch -D` | Force-deletes a branch. Capital `-D` means delete even if not merged |

---

### Phase 5: Staging and Committing

#### `git add -A`
```bash
git add -A
```
**What it does:** Stages ALL changes — modified, new, and deleted files.

**Concept:** Staging = telling Git "include these changes in my next commit." Think of it as packing items into a box before shipping.

| Variant | What it stages |
|---------|---------------|
| `git add -A` | Everything (modified + new + deleted) |
| `git add .` | Everything in current directory and below |
| `git add file.ts` | Only that specific file |

---

#### `git commit -m "message"`
```bash
git commit -m "feat: Add Scheme PDF Upload and RAG Pipeline

- Add Gemini embedding integration (gemini-embedding-001)
- Add PDF processing service (fetch, extract, chunk, embed, store)
- Add scheme routes, controller, and models
- Add Cloudinary PDF upload middleware
- Add scheme validation rules
- Fix module import paths to use relative paths"
```

**What it does:** Creates a permanent snapshot of your staged changes with a descriptive message.

**Concept:** A commit is like a save point. You can always go back to any commit.

**Commit message format (Conventional Commits):**
```
<type>: <short description>     ← first line (subject)
                                ← blank line
- Bullet point details          ← body (optional)
- More details
```

| Type | When to use |
|------|-------------|
| `feat` | New feature |
| `fix` | Bug fix |
| `docs` | Documentation only |
| `refactor` | Code change that isn't a fix or feature |
| `chore` | Build/config changes |

---

### Phase 6: Pushing to GitHub

#### `git push -u origin feature/scheme-pdf-rag`
```bash
git push -u origin feature/scheme-pdf-rag
```

**What it does:** Uploads your commits to GitHub and creates the branch there.

**Breaking it down:**
| Part | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `git push` | Upload commits to remote |
| `-u` | Set "upstream tracking" (so future `git push` knows where to go) |
| `origin` | The remote name (GitHub) |
| `feature/scheme-pdf-rag` | The branch to push |

**After `-u` is set, future pushes are simply:**
```bash
git push   # Git remembers where to push
```

---

## Complete Flow Summary

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["1. git status<br/>Check what changed"] --> B["2. git remote -v<br/>Confirm GitHub connection"]
    B --> C["3. git branch<br/>See current branches"]
    C --> D["4. git stash<br/>Save changes temporarily"]
    D --> E["5. git checkout main<br/>Switch to main"]
    E --> F["6. git checkout -b new-branch<br/>Create new branch"]
    F --> G{"7. git stash pop<br/>Restore changes"}
    G -->|"❌ Conflict!"| H["8. git reset + clean<br/>Undo the mess"]
    H --> I["9. Switch back to<br/>voice-profile-ai"]
    I --> J["10. Delete bad branch<br/>git branch -D"]
    J --> K["11. git stash pop<br/>Restore changes ✅"]
    K --> L["12. git checkout -b<br/>Create branch from HERE"]
    L --> M["13. git add -A<br/>Stage all changes"]
    M --> N["14. git commit -m<br/>Save snapshot"]
    N --> O["15. git push -u origin<br/>Upload to GitHub 🚀"]

    G -->|"✅ Success"| M

    style G fill:#e76f51,color:#fff
    style H fill:#e76f51,color:#fff
    style O fill:#2d6a4f,color:#fff
```

---

## Key Takeaway: Why Branch FROM the Right Place

```
❌ WRONG: main → new-branch (missing voice-profile files)
✅ RIGHT: voice-profile-ai → new-branch (has all needed files)
```

Your scheme code **depends on** files created in `feature/voice-profile-ai` (like [Gemini.ts](file:///w:/NPTEL%20INTERNSHIP/Hackathon/NitiSetu/backend/src/config/Gemini.ts), `cloudinary.ts`, scheme models). So the new branch must start from that point, not from `main`!
